<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=electromagnetic-induction">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Electromagnetic induction</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1831">1831</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Electromagnetic induction is the production of an electromotive force across an electrical conductor in a changing magnetic field; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-faraday">Michael Faraday</a> is generally credited with its discovery, first demonstrated on 29 August 1831 with two wires wound on an iron ring, and Joseph Henry discovered it independently in 1832. Within two months Faraday generated a steady current by rotating a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> disc near a magnet. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-clerk-maxwell">James Clerk Maxwell</a> later described it mathematically, and it is used in transformers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electric-motor">electric motors</a> and generators.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/electromagnetic-induction.jpg" alt="Electromagnetic induction" width="320" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Faraday_ring_transformer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In his first experiment Faraday expected that when current began to flow in one wire a sort of wave would travel through the iron ring and cause an electrical effect on the opposite side. He saw a transient current, which he called a wave of electricity, when the battery was connected and another when it was disconnected.</p><p>Faraday explained induction with a concept he called lines of force, but scientists at the time widely rejected his theoretical ideas, mainly because they were not expressed mathematically. An exception was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-clerk-maxwell">James Clerk Maxwell</a>, who used them as the basis of his quantitative electromagnetic theory. Maxwell's differential equation for the time-varying case was called Faraday's law by Oliver Heaviside, and it is recognised today as one of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maxwell-s-equations">Maxwell's equations</a>. In 1834 Heinrich Lenz formulated the law that bears his name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Faraday's law says that the emf induced in a closed circuit equals the rate of change of the magnetic flux enclosed by the circuit. By Lenz's law the induced current flows in the direction that opposes the change which produced it. Winding the wire into a tight coil of N identical turns multiplies the emf by N. The law covers two different phenomena: the motional emf produced by a magnetic force on a moving wire, and the transformer emf produced by a changing magnetic field. Maxwell drew attention to the distinction in 1861, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a> noticed that both situations amount to relative movement between a conductor and a magnet, which was one of the principal paths that led him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theory-of-relativity">special relativity</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Induction underlies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electric-generator">electric generators</a>, in which mechanical energy is converted into electrical energy, and transformers, in which a changing current in one wire sets up an emf in a second. It is also used in inductors, induction motors, induction cooking and welding, inductive charging and magnetic flow meters for conductive liquids.</p><p>Conductors in a changing or moving magnetic field also carry circular eddy currents. These are useful in eddy current brakes and induction heating, but in the cores of transformers, motors and generators they waste energy as heat. Engineers reduce them by building cores from stacks of thin laminations separated by nonconductive coatings, or from ferrite or iron powder at higher frequencies.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Electromagnetic induction” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
